WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:06.940
The MRI scanner was invented in 1977, and
it's revolutionized medical diagnostics.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:12.370
Short for magnetic resonance imaging, an MRI
offers an inside look at the human body

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.410
without surgery or x rays.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.580
When investigating health problems, it's a
great way to get the picture.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:28.550
Using magnetic fields and radio wave pulses,
an MRI can look right through you to

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.010
determine what's going on under your skin.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.400
The magnet is incredibly powerful.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:39.850
It's up to 30,000 times stronger than the
Earth's magnetic field.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.670
To make an MRI scanner.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.560
They weld aluminum casing around the magnet,
creating a tunnel in the center for the

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:56.650
patient. The seams must be extremely tight
because the tube will also contain the super

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.460
cold liquid helium, which will make the
magnet so incredibly powerful.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:08.040
Once the welds are complete, they cap the
magnet and transfer it to a test chamber.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.800
They pump helium gas into the tube and
activate a vacuum system that sucks out the

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:16.850
air in the chamber.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.530
A sensor confirms there are no leaks and the
welds are tight.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.500
They now construct a second aluminum tube
around the magnet.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.170
This will act as an insulating shield.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:41.950
They wrap an aluminum mylar blanket tightly
around the magnet.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:47.530
Aluminum mylar was first developed as an
insulating material for spacesuits.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.330
In this case, it'll be used to deflect heat
to keep the magnet cold.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.020
They now insert the magnet into a steel
shell.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.780
This is known as the vacuum vessel because
air will be pulled from the space between it

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:08.550
and the magnet, creating a vacuum which will
serve as another insulator.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:20.840
They mask the tunnel with plastic and spray
paint the exterior white.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.620
The paint will protect the steel from rust.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:31.460
Next, they install a refrigeration unit
known as a cold head.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:38.300
The cold head will maintain the helium
around the MRI's primary magnet at the

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.890
incredibly chilly temperature of -269
degrees Celsius.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.580
This will keep the helium in a liquid state
without the cold head.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.360
Significant amounts would vaporize and be
lost.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.530
They now pump the liquid helium into the
magnet.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.050
It's one of the coldest things on the
planet.

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.170
And the film nozzles turn frosty.

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:10.240
Exposed to this extreme cold, the magnet
loses all electrical resistance and becomes a

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.300
superconductor, generating intense magnetic
fields.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:21.070
With the MRI magnet complete, they move on
to the gradient coil, which will control the

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.350
orientation of the image with electrical
pulses.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:29.200
It starts with a fiberglass epoxy tube cut
to the correct length.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.470
A worker carves grooves in the tube and
fills those grooves with epoxy.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:45.390
So he then winds copper wire into the epoxy
field grooves and it adheres forming the

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.500
gradient coil.

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:55.620
The team slathers epoxy onto the entire
tube.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.530
The epoxy seals the wire preventing
vibrations of the coil when it's in

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:10.020
operation. They layer etched copper plates
onto the epoxy and they adhere.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.220
They wrap the coil tightly with Teflon
cloth.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.870
A worker pours epoxy resin onto the fabric.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.590
He then winds tubing around the gradient
coil and it adheres to the epoxy coated

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.170
cloth. These are cooling lines.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:40.380
They'll disperse heat generated by the
gradient coil.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.340
Another worker now preps the next fiberglass
tube.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:49.090
This one is for the MRIs radio frequency
coil.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.900
There's much more to come before this MRI's
scanner is ready to provide an inside look at

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:56.950
the human body.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.690
Work on the MRIs.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.180
Radiofrequency coil is underway.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:06.100
A technician attaches plastic risers and
pads to the fiberglass core.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:11.500
The risers and pads will hold the circuitry
parts at a uniform level.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:17.290
She applies adhesive backed strips of copper
from one band of risers to the other.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:20.450
These copper strips are the antenna.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:25.660
They'll send and receive signals from the
body and relay them to a computer to produce

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.100
the MRI image.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:36.740
She presses the copper with a round tip tool
for better adhesion to the core.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.800
She then dabs epoxy glue into the riser
compartments.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.720
She inserts capacitors into the glue filled
compartments.

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:56.350
These little capacitors will store energy
and change the frequency of the coil to match

00:05:56.350 --> 00:06:04.450
the magnets. The technician sorters the ends
of the capacitors to the copper antenna as

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:05.830
she builds the circuitry.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:16.110
She transfers numerous high voltage inductor
boards to the pads and screws them in place.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.880
Next up, our cable splitter units.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.600
Another technician installs one near an
inductor board.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:36.480
She routes some of the cables from the
splitter to several of the inductors.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:45.000
And she solders the cables to the inductors.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:57.440
She places a plastic cable track on the side
of the radio frequency coil and snakes the

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.030
main power cable through it.

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:05.420
She saw it as the cable to the antenna.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:16.470
The technician stiffens the copper antenna
with composite boards to dampen vibrations

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:18.450
when the mry is in operation.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.090
She applies epoxy around all the cables.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:30.200
The epoxy secures the cables to keep them
from moving around and making noise.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:37.350
The MRI is radio frequency coil is complete
and ready to be put to the test.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:43.390
The next worker caps the ends with metal
domes and bolts them tightly to it.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:50.810
She pumps highly pressurized air into the
capped radio frequency coil.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:59.870
She sprays soapy water over the exterior of
the coil and looks for air bubbles.

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:06.260
Bubbles in the soapy residue would indicate
a leak that would disrupt airflow in the dry.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.930
With the radiofrequency coil leak free.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:14.870
They now slide it into the gradient coil,
which has gained an outer fiberglass shell

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:16.370
since we last saw it.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:27.350
The worker connects exterior tubing to the
network of cooling lines inside the gradient

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:34.630
coil. With those connections made, they're
ready to assemble the coils to the magnet.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.760
The worker slides the assembly off the cart
and into the MRI magnet.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:45.880
He installs a bracket that centers the
coils, maintaining a small gap between them

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:52.400
and the magnet. He equips the Maasai with
microphones and speakers to communicate with

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:58.790
the patient. There's also a signaling system
here for the patient to activate in the event

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:06.280
of an emergency. Now for the ultimate test.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:12.250
They energise the magnet and send standard
test objects into the MRI.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:17.260
They scan the objects and confirm that the
picture is clear.

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:22.120
They add a metal and plastic outer casing.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:25.690
This MRI's scanner is now ready to focus on
diagnosis.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.840
The next picture it takes could be a life
saver.

